/// Chapter 32 of the guide, written from md-docs/32-javascript-interop.md by
/// scripts/site-from-guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page InteropGuide(path: "/docs/guide/interop", title: "JavaScript interop", description: "Your own .js files under src/, libraries via meta.scripts, mount and cleanup on any element, wf:render, Element for custom elements, and publishing yours.", layout: DocsShell("JavaScript interop", chapter: t("guide.chapter", { n: "32" }), blurb: "Write plain JavaScript beside your pages and call it by name; load a library from a CDN; hand an element to a script and get it back; place custom elements; publish your components for any framework.", source: "md-docs/32-javascript-interop.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "your-own-script", label: "Your own script"), Anchor(id: "a-library-from-a-cdn", label: "A library from a CDN"), Anchor(id: "mount-update-cleanup-on-any-element", label: "mount:, update:, cleanup: — on any element"), Anchor(id: "wf-render-for-a-script-that-finds-elements-itself", label: "wf:render — for a script that finds elements itself"), Anchor(id: "somebody-else-s-custom-element", label: "Somebody else's custom element"), Anchor(id: "publishing-your-components-as-custom-elements", label: "Publishing your components as custom elements"), Anchor(id: "wf-from-a-script-of-your-own", label: "WF from a script of your own"), Anchor(id: "coming-from-external", label: "Coming from external")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/performance", prevLabel: "31. {t("ch.31")}", nextTo: "/docs/guide/pdf-and-slides", nextLabel: "33. {t("ch.33")}")) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("WebFluent has no package manager of its own, and does not need one to use the JavaScript ecosystem. A `.js` file is part of the project the way a `.css` file is: put it under `src/` and its functions are there.")

        Table(caption: "JavaScript interop") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("You want to…").header
                    Table.Cell("Use").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("call JavaScript of your own")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("a `.js` file under `src/`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("use a library from a CDN")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`meta.scripts` in the config") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("hand an element to a script, and take it back")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`mount:`, `update:`, `cleanup:` on any element") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("let a script find elements itself")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("the `wf:render` event") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("place somebody else's custom element")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Element(\"tag-name\", …)`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("use your components from React, Vue or a plain page")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`output_type: \"elements\"`") }
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("Your own script", id: "your-own-script").h2

        Markdown("A plain browser script, written as any `<script src>` file is written — no `export`, no `import`, no build step:")

        CodeBlock(#"// src/tilt.js
/**
 * Tilt an element toward the pointer.
 * @param {HTMLElement} node
 * @param {number} [max] how far, in degrees
 */
function initTilt(node, max = 15) {
  const move = (e) => {
    const r = node.getBoundingClientRect();
    const x = (e.clientX - r.left) / r.width - 0.5;
    node.style.transform = `rotateY(${x * max}deg)`;
  };
  node.addEventListener("pointermove", move);
  return {
    setMax(m) { max = m; },
    destroy() { node.removeEventListener("pointermove", move); },
  };
}"#, title: "js")

        CodeBlock(#"page Gallery(path: "/gallery", title: "Gallery", description: "Cards that lean.") {
    state max = 15

    Heading("Gallery").h1
    Card(class: "tilt",
         mount: (n) => initTilt(n, max),
         update: (t) => t.setMax(max),
         cleanup: (t) => t.destroy()) {
        Text("Move the pointer over me.")
    }
    Slider(bind: max, min: 0, max: 45, label: "How far")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- **The file runs as written.** It is copied to `build/js/tilt.js` byte for byte and linked with `<script defer>` on every page, before the compiled code — so a stack trace points at your line.\n- **Its names are in scope.** Every top-level `function`, `class`, `const`, `let` and `var`, and anything assigned to `window.x`, is a name `.wf` code calls directly. What a function or an IIFE keeps inside stays private, as it does in the browser.\n- **A call is checked.** `initTilt(n, 1, 2)` is a `T10` where it is written — the script takes one or two arguments — and its JSDoc types the rest (below). Nothing about it is a guess: a name the file does not declare is still a `T13`.\n- **A class is constructed.** WebFluent has no `new`; a call to a class a script declares compiles to one.\n- **One name, one meaning.** Two scripts declaring one name, or a script name that is also a component, store, `const` or a name the language or the browser owns (`format`, `log`, `fetch`), is an error naming both.\n- **An ES module is refused.** A file with a top-level `import` or `export` cannot run from a `<script src>`, so the build stops on it and says so. A published module is loaded from the config (below).\n\n`wf serve` rebuilds when a script changes, `wf audit` lists what each one declares, `wf types` prints each function's signature, and the editor completes, documents and jumps to them.")

        Heading("Types from JSDoc", id: "types-from-jsdoc").h3

        Markdown("JSDoc is a comment, so the file stays exactly what the browser runs. The checker reads it:")

        Table(caption: "Types from JSDoc") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("JSDoc").header
                    Table.Cell("WebFluent").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`string` · `number` · `boolean`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`String` · `Number` · `Bool`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`object` · `\{ a: T \}`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Map`, or a shape of the fields written") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`T[]` · `Array<T>`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`[T]`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`?T` · `T | null` · `[name]`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`T?`, or a parameter a call may leave out") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Promise<T>`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`T`, as an action that awaits it reads it") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`(v: T) => R`") }
                    Table.Cell("a function — an action or a lambda may be passed")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`HTMLElement`, anything else") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Any`") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("With no comment at all every parameter is `Any`, so adding a script never breaks a build; each tag only narrows what a call may pass.")

        Heading("A script reports; WebFluent owns the class", id: "a-script-reports-webfluent-owns-the-class").h3

        Markdown("When how an element looks depends on something only a script can see — an intersection, a scroll position, a library's event — hand the script an action. An action is a plain function at run time, and the class stays a `class:` the compiler can read:")

        CodeBlock(#"// src/visible.js
/**
 * Say whether `node` is on screen, each time that changes.
 * @param {HTMLElement} node
 * @param {(v: boolean) => void} onChange
 */
function watchVisible(node, onChange) {
  const io = new IntersectionObserver(([e]) => onChange(e.isIntersecting));
  io.observe(node);
  return { destroy: () => io.disconnect() };
}"#, title: "js")

        CodeBlock(#"page Story(path: "/story", title: "Story", description: "It fades in.") {
    state visible = false
    action setVisible(v: Bool) { visible = v }

    Heading("Story").h1
    Card(class: { "is-visible": visible },
         mount: (n) => watchVisible(n, setVisible),
         cleanup: (w) => w.destroy()) {
        Text("Here I am.")
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("One owner per class: a class `class:` names is WebFluent's, and a class a script adds with `classList.add` is the script's — `class:` only ever takes off what it put on.")

        Heading("A library from a CDN", id: "a-library-from-a-cdn").h2

        Markdown("The compiler never fetches anything, so it cannot read a file on another origin. A library is a URL the config lists, and your own scripts use it:")

        CodeBlock(#"{ "meta": {
    "scripts": ["https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/chart.js@4.4.1/dist/chart.umd.min.js"],
    "integrity": {
      "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/chart.js@4.4.1/dist/chart.umd.min.js": "sha384-…"
    }
} }"#, title: "json")

        CodeBlock(#"// src/charts.js
/**
 * A bar chart of `values`, one bar a quarter.
 * @param {HTMLCanvasElement} canvas
 * @param {number[]} values
 */
function drawSales(canvas, values) {
  return new Chart(canvas, {
    type: "bar",
    data: { labels: ["Q1", "Q2", "Q3", "Q4"], datasets: [{ label: "Sales", data: values }] },
  });
}"#, title: "js")

        CodeBlock(#"page Sales(path: "/sales", title: "Sales", description: "How it is going.") {
    state values: [Number] = [12, 19, 7, 15]

    Heading("Sales").h1
    Host(tag: "canvas",
         mount: (c) => drawSales(c, values),
         cleanup: (chart) => chart.destroy())
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- **Order.** Each entry is linked with `<script defer>` in the order listed, before your own scripts and the compiled code, so a library is there when the code that uses it runs.\n- **Out of order.** `\{ \"src\": \"…\", \"async\": true \}` loads a script nothing on the page waits for — analytics, a tag manager — with `<script async>`. Deferred scripts run in order, so a large one listed plainly would hold the compiled code, and the page's first full paint, until it had arrived; an `async` one runs whenever it arrives instead. A module, or a script whose `globals` `.wf` calls, cannot be `async` (`E111`).\n- **Integrity.** `meta.integrity` pins the file; a library from another origin without a hash draws a warning.\n- **The policy.** The Content-Security-Policy is widened by exactly the origins listed, so a declared library is never blocked by the policy that ships beside it.\n- **A module.** `\{ \"src\": \"https://…/confetti.mjs\", \"module\": true, \"as\": \"Confetti\" \}` imports an ES module and puts its exports on `window.Confetti`. Most CDNs also serve a plain build, which needs neither.\n- **Calling it from `.wf` directly.** `\{ \"src\": \"…\", \"globals\": [\"Chart\"] \}` puts the names a library defines in scope, each `Any` — the compiler cannot see their shape. A class among them is constructed when called.")

        Heading("mount:, update:, cleanup: — on any element", id: "mount-update-cleanup-on-any-element").h2

        Markdown("Every element and every component call takes the three:\n\n- `mount: (node) => …` runs each time the element is made — the first paint, a route change, an `if` branch arriving, a list item added. What it returns is the handle the other two get.\n- `update: (handle) => …` runs again whenever the state it reads changes.\n- `cleanup: (handle) => …` runs when the element leaves — its page, its branch or its list item — so nothing a library made outlives it.\n- On a component call they belong to the component's root element, unless the component declares a prop of that name itself.\n- A mount that throws takes itself out and logs the error; the page stays.\n\n`Host` is the same lifetime on a bare node: `Host(tag: \"canvas\", …)` makes the element a library wants — `div` by default, or `span`, `canvas`, `svg`, `section`, `figure`, `pre`, `p`, `ul`, `table`.")

        Heading("wf:render — for a script that finds elements itself", id: "wf-render-for-a-script-that-finds-elements-itself").h2

        Markdown("The page is drawn after your scripts run, drawn again over a pre-rendered one, and drawn anew on every route, so a script that does its own `querySelectorAll` listens for the drawing to land:")

        CodeBlock(#"// src/tilt-all.js
document.addEventListener("wf:render", (e) => {
  // e.detail.route is the route just drawn, e.detail.params its parameters.
  for (const el of document.querySelectorAll(".tilt-me:not([data-tilted])")) {
    el.dataset.tilted = "";
    initTilt(el);
  }
});"#, title: "js")

        Markdown("It fires once per drawing, not for an `if` branch or a list item arriving later — that is what `mount:` is for.")

        Heading("Somebody else's custom element", id: "somebody-else-s-custom-element").h2

        CodeBlock(#"page Pricing(path: "/pricing", title: "Pricing", description: "Plans.") {
    state loaded = false
    Heading("Pricing").h1
    Element("stripe-pricing-table", publishableKey: "pk_live_…", pricing-table-id: "prctbl_1") {
        on ready { loaded = true }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("`Element` places any custom element by its tag — lower case, with a hyphen, as every custom element's is. A named argument is an attribute (`publishableKey` is written `publishable-key`) and follows state like any other value; an `on` handler hears the events it fires, whatever their names; the block is its children; `class:`, `style \{ \}` and `mount:` work as on anything else. The script that defines it — your own, or one in `meta.scripts` — upgrades it when it runs.")

        Heading("Publishing your components as custom elements", id: "publishing-your-components-as-custom-elements").h2

        CodeBlock(#"{ "build": { "output_type": "elements", "elements": ["PriceTag", "Rating"] } }"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("Each name becomes a standards-based custom element: `PriceTag` is `<price-tag>`, and a one-word component is prefixed so its tag has the hyphen a custom element needs (`Rating` is `<wf-rating>`). The build writes `elements.js`, `styles.css` and a page listing the tags it published.")

        CodeBlock(#"<script src="/elements.js" defer></script>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/styles.css">

<price-tag label="Pro" amount="29" sale></price-tag>"#, title: "html")

        Markdown("An attribute is a prop, read when the element is connected and again whenever it changes: a `Number` prop is read as a number, a `Map` or a list as JSON, and a `Bool` is true when the attribute is present — as HTML reads `disabled` — and false when it is `=\"false\"`, so a framework that writes the string still works. An event the component declares is dispatched as a `CustomEvent` that bubbles. What the component created goes when the element leaves the document.\n\nReact, Vue, Svelte, Angular, Rails, WordPress and a plain page can all place a tag. That is the whole reason this is the answer rather than an adapter per framework: **the shared interface between frameworks is the platform.**")

        Heading("WF from a script of your own", id: "wf-from-a-script-of-your-own").h2

        Markdown("The runtime is `window.WF`: a script under `src/`, the console, or a library's callback can use it — `WF.navigate(\"/about\")`, `WF.toast(\"Saved\", \"success\")`, `WF.storeSnapshot(\"Cart\")`. The [runtime reference](/docs/guide/runtime) lists every function.\n\nTwo things to know:\n\n- A build keeps only the runtime modules its own code reaches. A script that calls a module nothing else uses needs `\"build\": \{ \"runtime\": \"full\" \}`.\n- A script from another origin must be listed in `meta.scripts`, which widens the policy for it. An inline `<script>` is refused by the build's CSP check ([Security](/docs/guide/security#content-security-policy)).")

        Heading("Coming from external", id: "coming-from-external").h2

        Markdown("WebFluent 4.1 described JavaScript with `external` declarations; 4.2 removed them. `wf migrate` rewrites a project: a remote module becomes a `meta.scripts` entry (its calls keep working, typed `Any`), a local module becomes a plain script under `src/` with its signatures as JSDoc and `X.f(…)` rewritten to `f(…)`, and `external element` call sites become `Element(\"tag-name\", …)`.")
    }
}
